---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/base-layout.astro';

const updated = '8 September 2026';

const heading = 'mt-12 text-xs font-medium tracking-widest text-ink-muted uppercase';
const body = 'mt-4 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted';
const list = 'mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted';
const code = 'rounded bg-card px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.9em] text-ink';
const link = 'underline underline-offset-4 transition-colors hover:text-ink';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Hoptick — Privacy Policy"
  description="Hoptick keeps everything on your own device: no servers, no accounts, no analytics."
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight">Hoptick — Privacy Policy</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-muted">Last updated: {updated}</p>

  <p class={body}>
    Hoptick is a browser extension that times how long you spend in a chosen context. It is
    designed so that your data never leaves your computer.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-8 rounded-xl border border-line bg-card p-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted">
    <strong class="font-medium text-ink">The short version:</strong> Hoptick has no servers, no
    accounts, and no analytics. It makes no network requests of any kind. Nothing you do in
    Hoptick is transmitted anywhere, and no data is ever sold or shared with anyone.
  </div>

  <h2 class={heading}>What Hoptick stores</h2>
  <p class={body}>
    Hoptick saves the following on your own device only, using the browser&apos;s <code
      class={code}>storage.local</code
    > API. This storage is local to the device and is not synced to your Google account or to any
    other browser you use.
  </p>
  <ul class={list}>
    <li>Which mode is active (Classic or Focus)</li>
    <li>Which Focus type is selected (No distractions or Deep work)</li>
    <li>The list of website domains you have chosen for each Focus type</li>
    <li>Your stopwatch state — elapsed time, and whether it is running or paused</li>
    <li>Your preference for whether timing should pause when the browser is not focused</li>
  </ul>
  <p class={body}>
    Uninstalling Hoptick removes all of it. You can also clear your site lists at any time from
    within the extension.
  </p>

  <h2 class={heading}>How Hoptick uses the address of your current tab</h2>
  <p class={body}>
    In Focus mode, Hoptick needs to know which site you are currently looking at in order to
    decide whether the stopwatch should be running or paused. To do this, it reads the domain name
    of your active tab — for example <code class={code}>youtube.com</code> — and compares it
    against the list of sites you chose.
  </p>
  <p class={body}>
    That domain is used for the comparison and then discarded. Hoptick does <strong
      class="font-medium text-ink">not</strong
    > record your browsing history, does not keep a log of the sites you visit, does not read the
    content of any page, and does not store the domain anywhere. Only the sites you explicitly
    added to your own lists are saved, because you typed them in yourself.
  </p>
  <p class={body}>In Classic mode, Hoptick does not look at your tabs at all.</p>

  <h2 class={heading}>Permissions, and why each one is needed</h2>
  <ul class={list}>
    <li>
      <code class={code}>storage</code> — to remember your settings, your site lists, and your
      elapsed time between sessions, so the stopwatch survives closing the popup or restarting the
      browser.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code class={code}>alarms</code> — to refresh the timer shown on the toolbar icon while the
      stopwatch is running. Browsers suspend idle extensions, and this keeps the displayed time
      correct.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code class={code}>tabs</code> — to read the domain of the active tab in Focus mode, as
      described above. This is what allows the stopwatch to start and stop on its own.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class={heading}>Data sharing and transfer</h2>
  <p class={body}>
    Hoptick does not collect, transmit, sell, or share any user data with any party. There are no
    third-party services, no advertising, no tracking, and no analytics of any kind embedded in the
    extension.
  </p>

  <h2 class={heading}>Limited Use</h2>
  <p class={body}>
    Hoptick&apos;s use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the <a
      href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/webstore/program-policies/limited-use"
      rel="noopener"
      class={link}>Chrome Web Store User Data Policy</a
    >, including the Limited Use requirements.
  </p>

  <h2 class={heading}>Children</h2>
  <p class={body}>
    Hoptick is not directed at children and collects no personal information from anyone,
    regardless of age.
  </p>

  <h2 class={heading}>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p class={body}>
    If this policy changes, the updated version will be posted on this page with a revised date
    above. Continued use of Hoptick after a change means you accept the updated policy.
  </p>

  <h2 class={heading}>Contact</h2>
  <p class={body}>
    Questions about this policy or about Hoptick&apos;s handling of data can be sent to <a
      href="mailto:shippedbypetr@gmail.com"
      class={link}>shippedbypetr@gmail.com</a
    >.
  </p>

  <footer class="mt-16 border-t border-line pt-6 text-sm text-ink-muted">
    <p>
      Hoptick is an independent project, built and maintained by one developer. This policy covers
      the Hoptick browser extension wherever it is published.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4">
      <a href="/" class={link}>Back home</a>
    </p>
  </footer>
</BaseLayout>
